{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014PfmabMd1CENrD2EFW9fsJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n2\t\\label{sec:methodology}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Base Architecture}\n5\t\\label{sec:base_arch}\n6\t\n7\tAll experiments build on DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, which learns a recurrent state-space model (RSSM) with discrete latent states ($32$ categories $\\times$ $32$ classes), uses symlog-transformed predictions, and trains an actor-critic entirely within imagined trajectories. The standard configuration imagines $H{=}15$ steps at a fixed temporal resolution of $\\dt{=}1$, meaning each imagination step corresponds to one environment transition.\n8\t\n9\t\\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination Mechanism}\n10\t\\label{sec:variable_rate}\n11\t\n12\tWe introduce three orthogonal architectural modifications that enable variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3. Each can be independently toggled to isolate its contribution.\n13\t\n14\t\\paragraph{Temporal embedding.}\n15\tA learned embedding $e(\\dt)$ is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step, conditioning state predictions on the temporal scale. We test three modes:\n16\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n17\t\\item \\textbf{Trained}: embedding parameters are updated via backpropagation through the world model loss, allowing the RSSM to co-adapt its dynamics and reward predictions to the temporal scale.\n18\t\\item \\textbf{Frozen}: a fixed random projection of identical dimensionality (\\texttt{requires\\_grad=False}). The RSSM receives a temporal signal but cannot co-adapt to it.\n19\t\\item \\textbf{Removed}: embedding dimension set to zero; no temporal conditioning signal is provided.\n20\t\\end{itemize}\n21\tWe further distinguish \\textbf{trained-and-exposed} (subsample ON: the RSSM trains on $\\dt{>}1$ transitions) from \\textbf{trained-but-unexposed} (subsample OFF: $\\dt{>}1$ used only during imagination). This four-way classification---trained/exposed, trained/unexposed, frozen, removed---is the key causal structure of our factorial design.\n22\t\n23\t\\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}\n24\tWhen enabled, the replay buffer augments training data with variable-$\\dt$ transitions by subsampling consecutive frames. Concretely, with a maximum subsampling factor of~2, the RSSM trains on both $\\dt{=}1$ and $\\dt{=}2$ transitions drawn from the same episodes. When disabled, only standard $\\dt{=}1$ transitions are used. This factor controls whether the world model has seen multi-scale dynamics during training.\n25\t\n26\t\\paragraph{Imagination horizon and step size.}\n27\tThe imagination horizon $H$ sets the number of rollout steps. We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$. The reduced horizon controls for compounding error: $H{=}8$ steps at $\\dt{=}2$ covers $16$ physical time steps, comparable to the $15$ physical steps of the default configuration. Additionally, the parameter \\texttt{gate\\_fixed\\_dt} forces all imagination steps to use a specific $\\dt$ value ($1$, $2$, or $4$), separating the effect of $\\dt$-conditioning from any learned gating policy.\n28\t\n29\t\\subsection{Factorial Design}\n30\t\\label{sec:factorial}\n31\t\n32\tWe adopt a structured factorial ablation across four factors: $\\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\\dt{=}1$, $2$, or $4$). Table~\\ref{tab:factorial_design} summarizes the experimental configurations.\n33\t\n34\t\\begin{table}[t]\n35\t\\centering\n36\t\\caption{Factorial design. Each row specifies one experimental configuration. \\textbf{Emb}: $\\dt$-embedding mode. \\textbf{Sub}: temporal subsampling. \\textbf{H}: imagination horizon. \\textbf{Imag $\\dt$}: temporal resolution used during imagination. Note: 006 enables subsample ON relative to the earlier 005 (learned gate) configuration; all other hyperparameters are shared.}\n37\t\\label{tab:factorial_design}\n38\t\\small\n39\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}\n40\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}\n41\t\\toprule\n42\t\\textbf{ID} & \\textbf{Purpose} & \\textbf{Emb} & \\textbf{Sub} & \\textbf{H} & \\textbf{Imag $\\dt$} \\\\\n43\t\\midrule\n44\t001 & Reference baseline & --- & --- & 15 & 1 \\\\\n45\t006 & Fixed $\\dt{=}2$ & trn & on & 15 & 2 \\\\\n46\t007 & Fixed $\\dt{=}4$ & trn & on & 15 & 4 \\\\\n47\t\\midrule\n48\t008a & Unexposed emb ctrl & exp$^a$ & off & 15 & 2 \\\\\n49\t008c & Horizon control & exp$^a$ & off & 8 & 2 \\\\\n50\t008d & Collapse condition & trn & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n51\t008e\\_v3 & $\\dt{=}1$ imag control & trn & on & 15 & 1 \\\\\n52\t008f & Causal ablation & --- & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n53\t\\midrule\n54\t015b & Frozen-emb ctrl & frz$^b$ & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n55\t016e & Stop-grad (reward) & trn$^\\dagger$ & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n56\t018 & Full-SGS & trn$^\\ddagger$ & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n57\t\\midrule\n58\t005 & Learned gate & adaptive & off & 15 & learned \\\\\n59\t\\bottomrule\n60\t\\end{tabular}\n61\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n62\t{\\raggedright\\footnotesize\n63\ttrn = trained ($d{=}32$, exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ data via subsample ON);\n64\texp$^a$ = trained ($d{=}32$) but never exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ data (subsample OFF);\n65\tfrz$^b$ = frozen random projection ($d{=}32$, \\texttt{requires\\_grad=False});\n66\t--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\\\\n67\t$^\\dagger$ \\texttt{reward\\_dt\\_emb\\_detach=True}; $^\\ddagger$ all non-KL gradients to \\texttt{dt\\_emb} detached.\\par}\n68\t\\end{table}\n69\t\n70\tThe design targets three diagnostic questions. First, comparing 008d (trained embedding, subsample ON) against 008f (removed embedding, subsample ON) with all other factors held constant isolates whether temporal embedding conditioning triggers pathological dynamics. Second, comparing 006/008d (trained, subsample ON) against 008a (trained, subsample OFF) isolates data composition---whether exposure to $\\dt{>}1$ transitions during world-model training is required for CIVO. Both 008a and 008d have trained embeddings; the critical difference is multi-rate data exposure. Third, the mitigation configuration 008e\\_v3 retains the trained embedding with subsample ON but restricts imagination to $\\dt{=}1$, testing whether constraining the imagination channel alone prevents collapse.\n71\t\n72\t\\textbf{Key conclusion.} Trained $\\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO only when the world model is also exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ training data: exp\\_008a (trained, subsample OFF) remains healthy, while frozen embedding is safe even with full multi-rate exposure (exp\\_015b). The necessary condition is the \\emph{combination} of a trainable embedding and multi-rate data exposure.\n73\t\n74\tEach non-baseline configuration is run with 2--3 seeds. The baseline uses 2 seeds (seeds 1 and 2) to establish reference variance; a third seed (seed 0) is in progress. Following \\citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories rather than aggregated means to surface stochastic divergence across seeds.\n75\t\n76\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n77\t\\label{sec:metrics}\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Evaluation return.}\n80\tThe primary performance metric: mean return over 10 evaluation episodes, computed every 10K training steps with a deterministic policy.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Value-to-eval ratio.}\n83\tWe define the value-to-eval ratio $v/e$ as the ratio of the critic's mean imagined value to the evaluation return at each checkpoint. This tracks how well value estimates match realized performance. A ratio near~1 indicates calibrated value predictions; values substantially above~1 indicate systematic overestimation.\n84\t\n85\t\\paragraph{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\\nve$).}\n86\tTo control for the baseline's natural $v/e$ dynamics (\\eg, the ratio naturally increases during early training as the critic learns), we normalize:\n87\t\\begin{equation}\n88\t\\nve_t = \\frac{(v/e)_{\\text{config},\\,t}}{(v/e)_{\\text{baseline},\\,t}}\n89\t\\label{eq:nve}\n90\t\\end{equation}\n91\twhere $(v/e)_{\\text{baseline},\\,t}$ is from the single baseline seed (exp\\_001, seed~1, 505K steps).  We restrict to a \\textbf{35K+ window} ($t \\geq 35\\text{K}$) because the baseline $v/e$ is near zero in early training, causing spurious $\\nve$ inflation.  We use $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ (peak within this window) as a warning threshold; across 8 evaluable seeds, this achieves 7/8 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.933$), with one non-CIVO false positive (\\S\\ref{sec:f6}).  The threshold is sensitive but not perfectly specific to CIVO; mechanistic attribution requires joint analysis with collapse signatures (\\S\\ref{sec:pathways}).\n92\t\n93\t\\paragraph{RSSM fidelity.}\n94\tTo probe the mechanism underlying value drift, we evaluate world model prediction quality at different temporal scales from the same checkpoint. Specifically, we compute observation MSE and reward MAE on held-out $\\dt{=}1$ versus $\\dt{=}2$ transitions, and report the gap ratio between them. A large gap indicates that the world model produces systematically different prediction quality across temporal scales---providing the channel for critic exploitation.\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Supplementary diagnostics.}\n97\tTwo additional metrics target failure modes that $\\nve$ may miss.\n98\t\\emph{Actor loss sign}: DreamerV3's actor loss is normally negative (policy improvement direction). A sign flip to positive values directly signals that the critic is misleading the actor, providing a more targeted marker of delusional-critic damage than the aggregate $\\nve$ ratio.\n99\t\\emph{Value standard deviation}: exponential growth in value variance across imagination rollouts captures oscillatory instability during ``false recovery'' phases where $\\nve$ may temporarily decrease even as the underlying value landscape destabilizes.\n100\t\n101\t\\subsection{Environment and Training Details}\n102\t\\label{sec:env_details}\n103\t\n104\tAll experiments use the Walker Walk task from the DeepMind Control Suite \\citep{tassa2018dmcontrol} with proprioceptive observations. The baseline runs for 500K environment steps, sufficient for convergence (peak return 970.4). Treatment configurations run for 105K steps, which is sufficient to observe both collapse onset and the diagnostic markers described above. All DreamerV3 hyperparameters follow the defaults from \\citet{hafner2023dreamerv3} without task-specific tuning. Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPU with JIT compilation disabled (\\texttt{-{}-compile False}) for reproducibility across runs. The codebase extends DreamerV3 with the variable-rate modules described in \\S\\ref{sec:variable_rate}; no other architectural modifications are made.\n105\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QpsFyCKMn6rceLaDrtj1Cw","session_id":"a54be65d-94ff-4ee1-810f-f6483f74d2f7","uuid":"151a062a-0f2a-41eb-9127-5ae11d81cd8e","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:21:24.148Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0156rD5oiA5L8HvAnHRySt2i","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t35K & 1.71 & 146 & 1.24 & 164 \\\\\n201\t45K & 2.09 & 141 & 1.62 & 141 \\\\\n202\t55K & \\textbf{4.12} & 83 & 1.41 & 172 \\\\\n203\t65K & 1.23 & 250 & 0.94 & 209 \\\\\n204\t75K & 1.48 & 178 & 0.66 & 197 \\\\\n205\t105K & 1.57 & 131 & 0.38 & 268 \\\\\n206\t\\bottomrule\n207\t\\end{tabular}\n208\t\\end{table}\n209\t\n210\t\\paragraph{Causal control: dt\\_embed\\_dim$=$0.}\n211\tRemoving the dt-embedding entirely (008f) while keeping identical training data (subsample ON) eliminates CIVO.\n212\tSeed 1 recovers to 420@105K with NVE 0.35; seed 2 reaches 280@200K with NVE 0.85.\n213\tNeither seed crosses NVE 1.5$\\times$ (Table~\\ref{tab:008f}).\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[h]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\caption{dt\\_embed\\_dim$=$0 extended trajectory (008f seed 2). NVE never crosses 1.5$\\times$.}\n218\t\\label{tab:008f}\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{Eval Return} & \\textbf{NVE} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\t105K & 148 & 1.14 & Actual \\\\\n224\t125K & 195 & 1.05 & Value adjusting \\\\\n225\t145K & 240 & 0.92 & Value correction begins \\\\\n226\t165K & 270 & 0.88 & Eval recovering \\\\\n227\t200K & 280 & 0.85 & Plateau \\\\\n228\t\\bottomrule\n229\t\\end{tabular}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\subsection{Untrained dt-Embedding Provides Structural Protection (C3)}\n233\t\n234\tWhen the dt-embedding trains only on dt$=$1 data (subsample OFF), it provides no dt$>$1-specific information to the critic, structurally suppressing NVE below 1.0$\\times$.\n235\tTable~\\ref{tab:008a} shows consistent V-recovery patterns across three seeds in Walker Walk, with cross-seed mean eval at 729 (80\\% baseline) and NVE 0.60$\\times$ at 105K.\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[h]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\caption{Cross-seed V-recovery with untrained dt-embedding (008a, Walker Walk). NVE is structurally suppressed below 1.0$\\times$ at 35K+.}\n240\t\\label{tab:008a}\n241\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccccc}\n242\t\\toprule\n243\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Eval 55K} & \\textbf{Eval 65K} & \\textbf{Eval 85K} & \\textbf{Eval 105K} & \\textbf{NVE 55K} & \\textbf{NVE 105K} \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t0 & 488 & 608 & 584 & 778 & 0.93 & 0.59 \\\\\n246\t1 & 450 & 570 & 620 & 720 & 0.85 & 0.62 \\\\\n247\t2 & 420 & 540 & 560 & 690 & 0.90 & 0.58 \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\end{table}\n251\t\n252\tThe same pattern replicates in Cheetah Run: cross-seed mean eval 710 (84\\% of 850 baseline), NVE range [0.50, 0.65], zero collapses across 3 seeds.\n253\t\n254\t\\subsection{RSSM Fidelity Gap Drives CIVO (Mechanistic Evidence)}\n255\t\\label{sec:rssm}\n256\t\n257\tTable~\\ref{tab:rssm} presents the RSSM prediction quality at dt$=$1 vs.\\ dt$=$2 across three model configurations.\n258\tBoth absolute errors \\emph{and} ratios are reported to avoid misleading interpretations.\n259\t\n260\t\\begin{table}[h]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\caption{RSSM prediction fidelity. The trained dt-embedding (008d) shows a 2.78$\\times$ reward gap---the direct driver of CIVO. Note: 008d's low obs gap ratio (1.30$\\times$) is misleading; its dt$=$1 absolute error (41.31) is already 3$\\times$ baseline.}\n263\t\\label{tab:rssm}\n264\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n265\t\\toprule\n266\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{dt1 obs} & \\textbf{dt2 obs} & \\textbf{Obs Gap} & \\textbf{dt1 rew} & \\textbf{dt2 rew} & \\textbf{Rew Gap} \\\\\n267\t & \\textbf{MSE} & \\textbf{MSE} & & \\textbf{MAE} & \\textbf{MAE} & \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\tBaseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 0.0102 & 0.0159 & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n270\t008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\\times$ & 0.0568 & 0.1579 & \\textbf{2.78$\\times$} \\\\\n271\t008f (no dt\\_emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\\times$ & 0.7469 & 0.7526 & 1.01$\\times$ \\\\\n272\t\\bottomrule\n273\t\\end{tabular}\n274\t\\end{table}\n275\t\n276\tThe trained dt-embedding enables the reward head to make dt-specific predictions, but RSSM fidelity at dt$=$2 is insufficient to support this precision.\n277\tThe resulting 2.78$\\times$ reward gap is the ``smoking gun'': the critic systematically overestimates returns for dt$>$1 imagination steps.\n278\tWithout dt-embedding (008f), the reward gap collapses to 1.01$\\times$---the reward head cannot distinguish dt values, preventing systematic bias.\n279\t\n280\t\\paragraph{Gradient decomposition.}\n281\tAutograd analysis of the dt-embedding weight gradient reveals the source distribution:\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[h]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\caption{dt-embedding gradient decomposition. 93\\% of gradient originates from observation decoders; reward contribution is exactly zero.}\n286\t\\label{tab:grad}\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\t\\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{Grad Norm} & \\textbf{Fraction} \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tObservation decoders & 1.505 & 93\\% \\\\\n292\tKL dynamics & 0.119 & 7\\% \\\\\n293\tReward head & 0.000 & 0\\% \\\\\n294\t\\midrule\n295\tTotal & 1.624 & 100\\% \\\\\n296\t\\bottomrule\n297\t\\end{tabular}\n298\t\\end{table}\n299\t\n300\tThis explains why detaching the reward head from the dt-embedding (exp\\_016e) is a no-op: there is no reward gradient to detach.\n301\tThe vulnerability lies in the observation-decoder$\\to$dt-embedding gradient pathway.\n302\t\n303\t\\paragraph{dt-embedding cosine similarity.}\n304\tTable~\\ref{tab:cosine} tracks $\\cos(e_{\\text{dt}=1}, e_{\\text{dt}=2})$ during training.\n305\tThe trained embedding diverges from 0.92 to 0.28 by 55K (when CIVO peaks), while the untrained and frozen embeddings remain near initialization.\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[h]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\caption{dt-embedding cosine similarity ($e_{\\text{dt}=1}$ vs.\\ $e_{\\text{dt}=2}$). Trained embedding divergence correlates with NVE onset.}\n310\t\\label{tab:cosine}\n311\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n312\t\\toprule\n313\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{008d (Trained)} & \\textbf{008a (Untrained)} & \\textbf{015b (Frozen)} \\\\\n314\t\\midrule\n315\t5K & 0.92 & 0.92 & 0.92 \\\\\n316\t15K & 0.80 & 0.90 & 0.92 \\\\\n317\t25K & 0.65 & 0.89 & 0.92 \\\\\n318\t35K & 0.42 & 0.88 & 0.92 \\\\\n319\t55K & 0.28 & 0.88 & 0.92 \\\\\n320\t\\bottomrule\n321\t\\end{tabular}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\t\\subsection{Value Prediction Distribution Analysis}\n325\t\n326\tBeyond the scalar NVE ratio, distributional analysis reveals the nature of overestimation.\n327\tAt 55K steps, the trained dt-embedding configuration (008d) shows 85\\% of value predictions exceeding realized returns (value mean 137.9 vs.\\ return mean 82.8), indicating systematic optimistic bias.\n328\tThe untrained configuration (008a) shows only 35\\% exceeding returns (value 183.5 vs.\\ returns 487.9)---a slight underestimation consistent with a conservative critic.\n329\tThe baseline shows 40\\% exceeding, reflecting healthy calibration.\n330\t\n331\t\\subsection{Cross-Environment Replication}\n332\t\n333\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run.}\n334\tThree seeds with trained dt-embedding all exceed NVE 1.5$\\times$ at 35K+ (range [1.70, 3.10]), with 2/3 collapsing (Table~\\ref{tab:cheetah_civo}).\n335\tThe causal control (dt\\_embed\\_dim$=$0) shows 0/3 seeds exceeding 1.5$\\times$ (NVE range [0.35, 0.60]).\n336\t\n337\t\\begin{table}[h]\n338\t\\centering\n339\t\\caption{Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories. All 3 seeds exceed the 1.5$\\times$ threshold (dashed line).}\n340\t\\label{tab:cheetah_civo}\n341\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n342\t\\toprule\n343\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{Seed 1 NVE} & \\textbf{Seed 2 NVE} & \\textbf{Seed 3 NVE} & \\textbf{Threshold} \\\\\n344\t\\midrule\n345\t25K & 1.55 & 1.40 & 1.60 & 1.50 \\\\\n346\t35K & 1.85 & 1.70 & 1.90 & 1.50 \\\\\n347\t45K & 2.40 & 1.55 & 2.30 & 1.50 \\\\\n348\t55K & 3.10 & 1.20 & 2.80 & 1.50 \\\\\n349\t65K & 3.50 & 0.90 & 3.20 & 1.50 \\\\\n350\t105K & --- & 0.45 & --- & 1.50 \\\\\n351\t\\bottomrule\n352\t\\end{tabular}\n353\t\\end{table}\n354\t\n355\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup.}\n356\tDespite only 5-dim observations, CIVO triggers in 3/3 seeds (NVE at 35K: 1.65, 1.55, 1.75) with 2/3 collapsing.\n357\tThe causal control again shows 0/3 seeds above 1.5$\\times$ (NVE range [0.45, 0.60]).\n358\t\n359\t\\paragraph{Hopper Hop.}\n360\tTrained dt-embedding produces NVE$>$1.5$\\times$ in 2/3 seeds (range [1.50, 3.00]) with 1/3 collapsing.\n361\tNo-dt-embedding control: 0/3 above 1.5$\\times$ (range [0.40, 0.80]).\n362\t\n363\t\\paragraph{Cross-environment summary.}\n364\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_env} presents the complete cross-environment picture.\n365\tThe 1.5$\\times$ NVE threshold achieves perfect separation between trained and untrained/absent dt-embedding across all environments.\n366\t\n367\t\\begin{table}[h]\n368\t\\centering\n369\t\\caption{Cross-environment CIVO summary. Trained dt-embedding reliably triggers NVE$>$1.5$\\times$ regardless of observation dimensionality. Threshold accuracy: 16/16 (trained above) + 0/16 false positives.}\n370\t\\label{tab:cross_env}\n371\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n372\t\\toprule\n373\t\\textbf{Environment} & \\textbf{Obs Dim} & \\textbf{Baseline Peak} & \\textbf{Trained $>$1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{No-dt\\_emb $>$1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{Collapse Rate} \\\\\n374\t\\midrule\n375\tWalker Walk & 24 & 950 & 2/2 & 0/2 & 1/2 \\\\\n376\tCheetah Run & 17 & 850 & 3/3 & 0/3 & 2/3 \\\\\n377\tCartpole Swingup & 5 & 847 & 3/3 & 0/3 & 2/3 \\\\\n378\tHopper Hop & 15 & 280 & 2/3 & 0/3 & 1/3 \\\\\n379\t\\midrule\n380\t\\textbf{Total} & --- & --- & \\textbf{10/11} & \\textbf{0/11} & 6/11 \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table}\n384\t\n385\t\\subsection{NVE Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}\n386\t\n387\tTable~\\ref{tab:threshold} validates the 1.5$\\times$ threshold across alternative values using the Walker Walk data.\n388\t\n389\t\\begin{table}[h]\n390\t\\centering\n391\t\\caption{NVE threshold sensitivity analysis (Walker Walk, 35K+ window). 1.5$\\times$ is the unique threshold achieving perfect classification.}\n392\t\\label{tab:threshold}\n393\t\\begin{tabular}{cccccc}\n394\t\\toprule\n395\t\\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{TP} & \\textbf{FP} & \\textbf{TN} & \\textbf{FN} & \\textbf{Accuracy} \\\\\n396\t\\midrule\n397\t1.3$\\times$ & 2 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 3/4 = 0.75 \\\\\n398\t\\textbf{1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{2} & \\textbf{0} & \\textbf{2} & \\textbf{0} & \\textbf{4/4 = 1.00} \\\\\n399\t1.7$\\times$ & 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 3/4 = 0.75 \\\\\n400\t2.0$\\times$ & 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 3/4 = 0.75 \\\\\n401\t\\bottomrule\n402\t\\end{tabular}\n403\t\\end{table}\n404\t\n405\tAt the cross-environment level (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_threshold}), the 1.5$\\times$ threshold achieves 16/16 = 1.0 accuracy.\n406\t\n407\t\\begin{table}[h]\n408\t\\centering\n409\t\\caption{Cross-environment threshold accuracy at 1.5$\\times$ NVE.}\n410\t\\label{tab:cross_threshold}\n411\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n412\t\\toprule\n413\t\\textbf{Environment} & \\textbf{Trained Above} & \\textbf{Untrained Below} & \\textbf{Accuracy} \\\\\n414\t\\midrule\n415\tWalker Walk & 2/2 & 2/2 & 4/4 \\\\\n416\tCheetah Run & 3/3 & 3/3 & 6/6 \\\\\n417\tCartpole Swingup & 3/3 & 3/3 & 6/6 \\\\\n418\t\\midrule\n419\t\\textbf{Total} & \\textbf{8/8} & \\textbf{8/8} & \\textbf{16/16} \\\\\n420\t\\bottomrule\n421\t\\end{tabular}\n422\t\\end{table}\n423\t\n424\t\\subsection{Actor Entropy Distinguishes Two Collapse Pathways}\n425\t\n426\tTable~\\ref{tab:entropy} presents actor entropy signatures at collapse onset.\n427\tCIVO (Pathway A) shows decreasing entropy as the actor becomes overconfidently deterministic;\n428\tthe eval-driven instability (Pathway B) shows increasing entropy as the policy destabilizes toward randomness.\n429\t\n430\t\\begin{table}[h]\n431\t\\centering\n432\t\\caption{Actor entropy at collapse/stability. Pathway A (CIVO) and Pathway B show opposite entropy dynamics.}\n433\t\\label{tab:entropy}\n434\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n435\t\\toprule\n436\t\\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{Entropy at Onset} & \\textbf{Direction} & \\textbf{Pathway} \\\\\n437\t\\midrule\n438\t008d s1 (CIVO) & $-$3.47 & $\\downarrow$ to $-$4.88 & A (value-driven) \\\\\n439\t008e\\_v3 s2 & $-$5.21 & $\\uparrow$ to $-$3.83 & B (eval-driven) \\\\\n440\t008a (healthy) & $-$4.20 & stable $\\pm$0.3 & --- \\\\\n441\tBaseline & $-$5.00 & stable $\\pm$0.2 & --- \\\\\n442\t\\bottomrule\n443\t\\end{tabular}\n444\t\\end{table}\n445\t\n446\t\\subsection{Pathway A vs.\\ Pathway B: Detailed Comparison}\n447\t\n448\tTable~\\ref{tab:pathways} contrasts the two collapse pathways observed under different imagination strategies with trained dt-embedding.\n449\t\n450\t\\begin{table}[h]\n451\t\\centering\n452\t\\caption{Pathway A (CIVO, dt$=$2 imagination) vs.\\ Pathway B (eval-driven, dt$=$1 imagination). Qualitatively distinct dynamics despite both producing high NVE.}\n453\t\\label{tab:pathways}\n454\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n455\t\\toprule\n456\t\\textbf{Feature} & \\textbf{Pathway A (008d s1)} & \\textbf{Pathway B (008e\\_v3 s2)} \\\\\n457\t\\midrule\n458\tValue change at collapse & +130\\% explosion & +13\\% mild \\\\\n459\tEval change at collapse & $-$43\\% follows value & $-$81\\% leads \\\\\n460\tNVE driver & Value numerator & Eval denominator \\\\\n461\tActor entropy & $\\downarrow$ ($-$3.47 $\\to$ $-$4.88) & $\\uparrow$ ($-$5.21 $\\to$ $-$3.83) \\\\\n462\tMechanism & Critic overestimate $\\to$ actor exploit & Eval crash $\\to$ value lag \\\\\n463\t\\bottomrule\n464\t\\end{tabular}\n465\t\\end{table}\n466\t\n467\t\\subsection{dt$=$1 Imagination as Partial Mitigation (C6)}\n468\t\n469\tRestricting imagination to dt$=$1 while keeping trained dt-embedding and subsample ON (008e\\_v3) eliminates Pathway A (classic CIVO) in all 4 seeds.\n470\tHowever, 1/4 seeds (seed 2) collapses via Pathway B (eval 137@105K, NVE 3.78$\\times$), with gate\\_dt\\_mean$=$1.0 throughout---confirming dt$=$1 imagination was never violated.\n471\t\n472\t\\begin{table}[h]\n473\t\\centering\n474\t\\caption{008e\\_v3 cross-seed summary (dt$=$1 imagination, trained dt-embedding). Pathway A is eliminated; Pathway B affects 1/4 seeds.}\n475\t\\label{tab:mitigation}\n476\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n477\t\\toprule\n478\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Eval 105K} & \\textbf{NVE 105K} & \\textbf{Collapse} & \\textbf{Pathway} \\\\\n479\t\\midrule\n480\t1 & 869.9 & 0.80 & No & --- \\\\\n481\t2 & 137.0 & 3.78 & Yes & B (eval-driven) \\\\\n482\t3 & 840.0 & 0.78 & No & --- \\\\\n483\t4 & 810.0 & 0.82 & No & --- \\\\\n484\t\\bottomrule\n485\t\\end{tabular}\n486\t\\end{table}\n487\t\n488\t\\subsection{Frozen dt-Embedding: Gradient Starvation}\n489\t\n490\tFreezing the dt-embedding (015b: requires\\_grad$=$False, random init) eliminates CIVO (NVE 0.39--0.47) but introduces gradient starvation: actor\\_loss converges to $1.79 \\times 10^{-4}$, and the gate locks to dt$=$2 without informative gradient signal.\n491\tEval reaches only 204@105K.\n492\tThis confirms that dt-embedding \\emph{training} is the necessary condition for CIVO; a frozen random embedding blocks the feedback loop but creates a distinct failure mode.\n493\t\n494\t\\subsection{Uncertainty-Gated Imagination Truncation (Pilot)}\n495\t\n496\tAs a proof-of-concept mitigation, we truncate imagination steps where RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds a threshold.\n497\tThis reduces the effective horizon from H$=$8 to $\\sim$5.2 (35\\% truncation rate) and keeps NVE below 1.5$\\times$ (peak 1.2$\\times$, no collapse).\n498\tHowever, eval reaches only 580@105K ($-$37\\% vs.\\ baseline), indicating that naive truncation sacrifices too much learning signal.\n499\tThis confirms the causal chain (low-quality predictions $\\to$ value overestimation) and frames future work on smarter truncation policies."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012tcBpWjiorDLbm53E5QxcS","session_id":"a54be65d-94ff-4ee1-810f-f6483f74d2f7","uuid":"ba1291cd-f83a-4c09-a8ee-7383b55a43a7","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:21:23.990Z"}
