{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M8CTYiK23vEgG8pqUTDg6g","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:results}\n3\t\n4\tWe organize results around seven findings (F1--F7) that establish a causal chain from trained temporal conditioning to pathological value dynamics and performance collapse, culminating in a gradient-level intervention that eliminates the vulnerability. All experiments use Walker Walk (DMC) with DreamerV3; treatments run to 105K steps and the baseline to 505K steps. We report evaluation returns (mean over 10 episodes) and the normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\\nve$), defined as the ratio of a configuration's imagined value to evaluation return, normalized by the baseline's ratio at the same training step.\n5\t\n6\t%% ============================================================\n7\t%% TABLE 1: Main results summary\n8\t%% ============================================================\n9\t\n10\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n11\t\\centering\n12\t\\caption{All configurations and outcomes. NVE reports peak $\\nve$ in the 35K+ window (\\S\\ref{sec:metrics}). CIVO-positive seeds are shaded; $\\dagger$\\,marks a false positive (non-CIVO collapse with elevated NVE).}\n13\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n14\t\\footnotesize\n15\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n16\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}\n17\t\\toprule\n18\t\\textbf{Exp} & \\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{dt\\_emb} & \\textbf{Sub} & \\textbf{H} & \\textbf{Eval@final} & \\textbf{NVE} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n19\t\\midrule\n20\t001 & Baseline ($\\dt{=}1$) & --- & --- & 15 & 915.7 @105K & 1.00$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n21\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n22\t006 & Fixed $\\dt{=}2$ & trn & ON & 15 & 132.9 @65K & 4.83$\\times$ & Collapsed \\\\\n23\t007 & Fixed $\\dt{=}4$ & trn & ON & 15 & 152.5 @55K & --- & Collapsed \\\\\n24\t005 & Learned gate & adaptive & OFF & 15 & 98.8 @145K & --- & Collapsed \\\\\n25\t\\midrule\n26\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n27\t008d & Factorial (s1) & trn & ON & 8 & 82.8 @55K & 4.12$\\times$ & Full collapse \\\\\n28\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n29\t009b & Factorial (s2) & trn & ON & 8 & 267.9 @105K & 1.62$\\times$ & Transient CIVO \\\\\n30\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n31\t009e & Factorial (s1) & trn & ON & 8 & 131.1 @105K & 4.12$\\times$ & Oscillatory \\\\\n32\t008f & No dt\\_emb (s1) & --- & ON & 8 & 503.0 @105K & 0.97$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n33\t009c & No dt\\_emb (s2) & --- & ON & 8 & 148.3 @105K & 1.14$\\times$ & Declining \\\\\n34\t008a & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 777.7 @105K & 0.93$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n35\t008c & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 8 & 240.9 @65K & 0.58$\\times$ & Slow learner \\\\\n36\t015b & Frozen emb & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 204.2 @105K & 0.47$\\times$ & Grad.\\ starvation \\\\\n37\t\\midrule\n38\t018 & Full-SGS & trn$^\\ddagger$ & ON & 8 & 915.7 @105K & 1.00$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n39\t008e\\_v3 s1 & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 869.9 @105K & 0.80$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n40\t008e\\_v3 s2$^\\dagger$ & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 137.0 @105K & 3.78$\\times$ & Non-CIVO collapse \\\\\n41\t009a & Baseline (s2) & --- & --- & 15 & 836.5 @105K & --- & Healthy \\\\\n42\t\\bottomrule\n43\t\\end{tabular}\n44\t\\vspace{1pt}\n45\t{\\raggedright\\footnotesize\n46\ttrn = trained ($d{=}32$, exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ data via subsample ON);\n47\ttrn$^\\ddagger$ = trained ($d{=}32$) with full-pathway SGS (all non-KL gradients detached from $e_{\\dt}$);\n48\texp$^a$ = trained ($d{=}32$) but never exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ data (subsample OFF);\n49\tfrz$^b$ = frozen random projection ($d{=}32$, \\texttt{requires\\_grad=False});\n50\t--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\par}\n51\t\\end{table*}\n52\t\n53\t%% ============================================================\n54\t%% F1: All dt>1 degrade\n55\t%% ============================================================\n56\t\n57\t\\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}\n58\t\\label{sec:f1}\n59\t\n60\tEvery configuration that introduces $\\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\\dt{=}1$ baseline, regardless of mechanism (Figure~\\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline (exp\\_001) reaches 915.7 at 105K steps and peaks at 970.4 at 505K. In contrast, fixed $\\dt{=}2$ (exp\\_006) peaks at 485.3 at 35K before collapsing to 132.9 by 65K---a $-73\\%$ decline from its own peak (14\\% of baseline). Fixed $\\dt{=}4$ (exp\\_007) peaks at only 176.9 at 25K and never exceeds 152.5 thereafter. The learned-gate configuration (exp\\_005) peaks at 541.2 at 55K but collapses to 98.8 by 145K. This uniform degradation motivates the factorial analysis that follows.\n61\t\n62\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n63\t\\centering\n64\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}\n65\t\\caption{Learning curves for all configurations. The $\\dt{=}1$ baseline (black) reaches 915.7 at 105K; all variable-rate configurations degrade, with fixed $\\dt{=}4$ and the learned gate collapsing most severely.}\n66\t\\label{fig:learning_curves}\n67\t\\end{figure}\n68\t\n69\t%% ============================================================\n70\t%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x\n71\t%% ============================================================\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Triggers Value Overestimation}\n74\t\\label{sec:f2}\n75\t\n76\tThe factorial design isolates trained $\\dt$-embedding as the factor that reliably triggers pathological value overestimation. The key causal control compares exp\\_008d (trained $\\dt$-embedding, subsampling ON, $H{=}8$) against exp\\_008f (identical configuration but \\emph{no} trained $\\dt$-embedding). As shown in Figure~\\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}:\n77\t\n78\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n79\t\\item \\textbf{008d} (trained): $\\nve = 1.78\\times$ at 25K $\\rightarrow$ $4.12\\times$ at 55K $\\rightarrow$ collapse (eval 167$\\rightarrow$82.8).\n80\t\\item \\textbf{008f} (no dt\\_emb): $\\nve = 1.02\\times$ at 25K $\\rightarrow$ $0.30\\times$ at 105K $\\rightarrow$ no collapse (eval 503.0 at 105K).\n81\t\\end{itemize}\n82\t\n83\tIn the 35K+ window (\\S\\ref{sec:metrics}), the $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ threshold correctly classifies 7/8 seeds (AUROC${=}0.933$, Clopper--Pearson 95\\% CI $[0.47, 1.0]$): 3 CIVO-positive (exp\\_006, 008d, 009b) and 4 CIVO-negative, with one non-CIVO false positive (008e\\_v3 s2, $3.78\\times$; \\S\\ref{sec:f6}).  The gap between the lowest true positive ($1.62\\times$) and the highest true negative ($1.00\\times$) is $0.62$.\n84\t\n85\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n86\t\\centering\n87\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig2_nve_trajectory.pdf}\n88\t\\caption{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\\nve$) trajectories. The dashed line marks the $1.5\\times$ threshold. All trained-embedding configurations with $\\dt > 1$ imagination (red/orange) exceed this threshold; the mitigation configuration (008e\\_v3 s1, dash-dot) with $\\dt{=}1$-only imagination remains below it.}\n89\t\\label{fig:nve_trajectory}\n90\t\\end{figure}\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Three post-CIVO outcomes.}\n93\tRunning the 008d configuration with two independent seeds (s1, s2) reveals that trained $\\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO---yet the downstream consequence is stochastic. Exp\\_009e extends seed~1 (008d) beyond 55K to 105K steps:\n94\t\n95\t%% ============================================================\n96\t%% TABLE 3: Three post-CIVO outcomes\n97\t%% ============================================================\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{table}[t]\n100\t\\centering\n101\t\\caption{Post-CIVO outcomes for the 008d configuration. Peak/Final NVE measured in the 35K+ window. Seeds 1 and 2 are independent runs; 009e extends seed~1 (008d) from 55K to 105K.}\n102\t\\label{tab:post_civo}\n103\t\\footnotesize\n104\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n105\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n106\t\\toprule\n107\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Peak} & \\textbf{Final} & \\textbf{Eval} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n108\t\\midrule\n109\ts1 (008d) & 4.12$\\times$ & 4.12$\\times$ & 82.8 & Full collapse \\\\\n110\ts2 (009b) & 1.62$\\times$ & 0.38$\\times$ & 267.9 & Transient CIVO \\\\\n111\ts1 ext (009e) & 4.12$\\times$ & 1.57$\\times$ & 131.1 & Oscillatory \\\\\n112\t\\bottomrule\n113\t\\end{tabular}\n114\t\\end{table}\n115\t\n116\t\\noindent\\textbf{Seed 1 (008d):} Full collapse (Pathway A). Value explosion ($+130\\%$), evaluation declines from 167 to 82.8, entropy decreases---the classic CIVO feedback loop.\n117\t\n118\t\\noindent\\textbf{Seed 2 (009b):} Transient CIVO. The $\\nve$ reaches $1.62\\times$ at 45K (35K+ window), exceeding the $1.5\\times$ threshold, but the value function self-corrects to $0.38\\times$ by 105K, and evaluation recovers to 267.9. This demonstrates the threshold's sensitivity to early CIVO signals even in cases that spontaneously recover.\n119\t\n120\t\\noindent\\textbf{Seed 1 extended (009e):} Oscillatory degradation. Continuing 008d beyond 55K, evaluation oscillates between 82.7 and 250 before settling at 131.1; the value function rises then reverses (peak $\\nve = 4.12\\times$ at 55K, final $1.57\\times$).\n121\t\n122\tAll runs exceed $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ in the 35K+ window, confirming deterministic CIVO triggering, but downstream outcomes are seed-dependent (1/2 persistent collapse rate among independent seeds).\n123\t\n124\t%% ============================================================\n125\t%% F3: Unexposed dt_embedding suppresses value drift\n126\t%% ============================================================\n127\t\n128\t\\subsection{F3: Unexposed Temporal Embedding Suppresses Value Drift}\n129\t\\label{sec:f3}\n130\t\n131\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n132\t\\centering\n133\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig3_factorial_grid.pdf}\n134\t\\caption{Factorial isolation of CIVO trigger. Only the combination of trained $e_{\\dt}$ and multi-rate data (upper-left cell) produces pathological NVE. Neither factor alone is sufficient.}\n135\t\\label{fig:factorial_grid}\n136\t\\end{figure}\n137\t\n138\tThe causal claim that trained embedding \\emph{plus} multi-rate exposure jointly trigger CIVO rests on two complementary controls that each remove one factor while holding the other constant.\n139\t\n140\t\\paragraph{Evidence A: exposure is necessary (exp\\_008a).}\n141\tExp\\_008a uses a trained embedding ($d{=}32$, gradients enabled) that is never exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ transitions during world-model training (subsample OFF). Despite sharing the same embedding architecture as exp\\_008d, its NVE remains in the $0.59$--$0.93\\times$ range across 105K steps. Performance follows two V-shaped recovery cycles, reaching 777.7 at 105K (85\\% of baseline). The critical trigger is not the embedding's capacity to represent temporal information, but its adaptation to multi-rate data that enables precise but inaccurate $\\dt{>}1$ state predictions within the RSSM.\n142\t\n143\tThis contrasts with exp\\_006 (trained $e_{\\dt}$, subsample ON, $H{=}15$), where NVE accelerates from $1.61\\times$ at 45K to $2.31\\times$ at 55K before catastrophic collapse to 132.9 at 65K. Both experiments use identical trained embeddings ($d{=}32$, gradients enabled) and $H{=}15$, differing only in whether the world model sees $\\dt{>}1$ transitions during training---confirming that multi-rate data exposure, not embedding architecture, is the operative variable.\n144\t\n145\t\\paragraph{Evidence B: trainability is necessary (exp\\_015b).}\n146\tExp\\_015b uses a frozen random-projection embedding ($d{=}32$, \\texttt{requires\\_grad=False}, subsample ON, $H{=}8$)---the same multi-rate data regime as exp\\_008d, but with a non-trainable temporal signal. Despite full exposure to $\\dt{>}1$ transitions, the NVE never exceeds the CIVO threshold: it remains in the $0.39$--$0.47\\times$ range throughout the 55K--105K window, with a brief peak of $1.45\\times$ at 55K that self-corrects to $0.65\\times$ by 65K. Final evaluation reaches only 204.2 at 105K---low, but through a qualitatively different failure mode than CIVO. Actor loss approaches zero (gradient starvation; see \\S\\ref{sec:pathways}), indicating that the frozen embedding provides no useful gradient signal for temporal adaptation.\n147\t\n148\tThe contrast with exp\\_008d is stark: under identical data conditions (subsample ON, $H{=}8$), the trained embedding drives NVE to $4.12\\times$ and triggers full collapse (eval 82.8), while the frozen embedding never exceeds $0.47\\times$ in the same window---an $8.7\\times$ NVE gap. This demonstrates that a $\\dt$-embedding must be able to \\emph{co-adapt} with RSSM dynamics to produce the systematic reward-prediction bias that drives CIVO; a static random projection, regardless of its exposure to multi-rate data, cannot create this bias.\n149\t\n150\tTogether, these two controls establish that CIVO requires the \\emph{joint} presence of (i)~a trainable embedding that can co-adapt with RSSM dynamics, and (ii)~multi-rate training data that drives this co-adaptation toward biased temporal predictions. Neither factor alone is sufficient.\n151\t\n152\t\\begin{table}[h]\n153\t\\centering\n154\t\\small\n155\t\\caption{Causal isolation: $e_{\\dt}$ configuration $\\times$ multi-rate data exposure. The combination of trained embedding and multi-rate exposure is the sole trigger for CIVO. Neither factor alone is sufficient.}\n156\t\\label{tab:causal_matrix}\n157\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n158\t\\toprule\n159\t& Multi-rate data (\\textsc{on}) & Single-rate data (\\textsc{off}) \\\\\n160\t\\midrule\n161\tTrained $e_{\\dt}$ & exp\\_008d: \\textbf{CIVO} & exp\\_008a: healthy \\\\\n162\t & NVE 4.12$\\times$, collapse & NVE 0.93$\\times$ \\\\\n163\t\\midrule\n164\tFrozen $e_{\\dt}$ & exp\\_015b: grad.\\ starvation & --- \\\\\n165\t & NVE 0.47$\\times$ & \\\\\n166\t\\midrule\n167\tRemoved $e_{\\dt}$ & exp\\_008f: no CIVO & --- \\\\\n168\t & NVE $\\leq$0.97$\\times$ & \\\\\n169\t\\bottomrule\n170\t\\end{tabular}\n171\t\\end{table}\n172\t\n173\tOnly the upper-left cell---trained embedding with multi-rate exposure---triggers CIVO (Table~\\ref{tab:causal_matrix}). This 2$\\times$2 structure establishes that both factors are jointly necessary.\n174\t\n175\t%% ============================================================\n176\t%% F4: dt=2 > dt=4 monotonic degradation\n177\t%% ============================================================\n178\t\n179\t\\subsection{F4: Degradation Scales Monotonically with \\texorpdfstring{$\\dt$}{dt}}\n180\t\\label{sec:f4}\n181\t\n182\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n183\t\\centering\n184\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig5_dt_amplification.pdf}\n185\t\\caption{Peak evaluation return as a function of imagination $\\dt$. Performance degrades monotonically: $\\dt{=}1$ (915.7) $>$ $\\dt{=}2$ (485.3, $-47\\%$) $>$ $\\dt{=}4$ (176.9, $-81\\%$).}\n186\t\\label{fig:dt_amplification}\n187\t\\end{figure}\n188\t\n189\tThe magnitude of degradation scales monotonically with the imagination step size (Figure~\\ref{fig:dt_amplification}). From the $\\dt{=}1$ baseline (915.7 at 105K), fixed $\\dt{=}2$ (exp\\_006) achieves a peak of only 485.3 ($-47\\%$), and fixed $\\dt{=}4$ (exp\\_007) peaks at 176.9 ($-81\\%$). This monotonic relationship is consistent with compounding model error: larger temporal gaps amplify the RSSM's prediction inaccuracy at each imagination step, and these errors accumulate across the rollout horizon.\n190\t\n191\t%% ============================================================\n192\t%% F5: Horizon and subsample secondary\n193\t%% ============================================================\n194\t\n195\t\\subsection{F5: Imagination Horizon and Subsampling Are Secondary Factors}\n196\t\\label{sec:f5}\n197\t\n198\tNeither horizon length nor temporal subsampling triggers collapse without multi-rate data exposure: both trained-but-unexposed configurations (008a, 008c) maintain $\\nve < 0.93\\times$ (\\S\\ref{sec:f3}), while the trained-and-exposed counterpart (008d) collapses with $\\nve = 4.12\\times$.  The no-embedding configurations show cross-seed variance (008f: 503.0 vs.\\ 009c: 148.3) but neither exhibits $\\nve > 1.5\\times$---confirming that low evaluation alone does not indicate CIVO.\n199\t\n200\tThe $3.2\\times$ performance gap (008a: 777.7 vs.\\ 008c: 240.9) quantifies the horizon effect independently of CIVO, as both conditions maintain healthy NVE ($\\leq$0.93$\\times$).\n201\t\n202\t%% ============================================================\n203\t%% F6: dt=1 imagination does not prevent collapse\n204\t%% ============================================================\n205\t\n206\t\\subsection{F6: Restricting Imagination to \\texorpdfstring{$\\dt{=}1$}{dt=1} Does Not Prevent Collapse}\n207\t\\label{sec:f6}\n208\t\n209\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n210\t\\centering\n211\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}\n212\t\\caption{Contrasting collapse dynamics. \\textbf{Left:} 008d (classic CIVO)---value explosion precedes evaluation decline, entropy decreases. \\textbf{Right:} 008e\\_v3 seed 2 (non-CIVO)---evaluation crashes while value remains stable, entropy \\emph{increases}. Baseline seed 2 (009a, dashed) remains stable at 836.5, ruling out seed fragility.}\n213\t\\label{fig:multiple_pathways}\n214\t\\end{figure}\n215\t\n216\tThe mitigation experiment exp\\_008e\\_v3 restricts imagination to $\\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained $\\dt$-embedding for observation conditioning. If CIVO were the sole vulnerability pathway, this restriction should prevent collapse. Results are mixed:\n217\t\n218\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n219\t\\item \\textbf{Seed 1} (008e\\_v3\\_s1): Healthy. Eval 869.9 at 105K ($95\\%$ of baseline), $\\nve = 0.80\\times$.\n220\t\\item \\textbf{Seed 2} (008e\\_v3\\_s2): Collapsed. Eval drops from 715 to 137 at 105K, $\\nve = 3.78\\times$.\n221\t\\end{itemize}\n222\t\n223\tThe baseline with seed 2 (exp\\_009a) is stable at 836.5 at 105K, ruling out inherent seed fragility as the explanation.\n224\t\n225\t\\paragraph{Distinct collapse mechanisms.}\n226\tThe collapse dynamics of 008e\\_v3\\_s2 differ qualitatively from the classic CIVO observed in 008d (Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}, Figure~\\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}):\n227\t\n228\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n229\t\\item \\textbf{008d (CIVO, Pathway A):} value explosion ($+130\\%$) $\\rightarrow$ evaluation decline $\\rightarrow$ entropy decreases.\n230\t\\item \\textbf{008e\\_v3\\_s2 (non-CIVO, Pathway B):} evaluation crashes ($-81\\%$) $\\rightarrow$ value remains stable $\\rightarrow$ entropy \\emph{increases} ($-5.21 \\rightarrow -3.83$).\n231\t\\end{itemize}\n232\t\n233\tIn Pathway A, the critic drives collapse through systematic overestimation; in Pathway B, the actor destabilizes independently while the critic remains initially well-calibrated. Restricting imagination to $\\dt{=}1$ prevents the CIVO mechanism---no early $\\nve$ spike, no reward amplification---but seed-dependent late-stage policy instability (Pathway B) can still occur.\n234\t\n235\tThis case also produces the sole false positive in the $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ classification: 008e\\_v3\\_s2's late evaluation decline inflates $\\nve$ to $3.78\\times$ through a non-CIVO mechanism (\\S\\ref{sec:pathways}).\n236\t\n237\t%% ============================================================\n238\t%% F7: Full-Pathway SGS Eliminates CIVO\n239\t%% ============================================================\n240\t\n241\t\\subsection{F7: Full-Pathway Gradient Stopping Eliminates CIVO}\n242\t\\label{sec:f7}\n243\t\n244\tExp\\_018 applies full-pathway selective gradient stopping (SGS), detaching all non-KL gradients from the temporal embedding $e_{\\dt}$ so that only the 7.3\\% KL dynamics signal remains. Under the same configuration that produces catastrophic CIVO in exp\\_008d (trained $e_{\\dt}$, $d{=}32$, subsample ON, $H{=}8$, $\\dt{=}2$ imagination), exp\\_018 reaches evaluation 915.7 at 105K steps---matching the baseline---with NVE $1.00\\times$ in the 35K+ diagnostic window, indistinguishable from the healthy $\\dt{=}1$ baseline. This directly contrasts with exp\\_008d's NVE of $4.12\\times$ and collapse to 82.8.\n245\t\n246\tThe progression across gradient-stopping interventions establishes a monotonic relationship between gradient removal and CIVO suppression:\n247\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n248\t\\item Exp\\_016e (reward-only SGS, 0\\% effective removal): NVE $10.42\\times$, collapse to 31.7---a null intervention since the reward head contributes zero gradient to $e_{\\dt}$.\n249\t\\item Exp\\_018 (full-pathway SGS, 92.7\\% removal): NVE $1.00\\times$, eval 915.7---CIVO completely eliminated.\n250\t\\item Exp\\_008f (embedding removed, 100\\% removal): NVE $0.97\\times$, eval 503.0---no CIVO, but lower performance due to loss of all temporal information.\n251\t\\end{itemize}\n252\t\n253\tNotably, exp\\_018 \\emph{outperforms} the no-embedding control (008f) by $1.82\\times$ in final evaluation (915.7 vs.\\ 503.0), demonstrating that the KL-only temporal signal provides useful dynamics information without inducing the pathological co-adaptation that drives CIVO. The observation decoder gradient pathway is necessary for CIVO induction; removing it eliminates the vulnerability within the 105K training horizon tested.\n254\t\n255\t%% ============================================================\n256\t%% TABLE 2: RSSM Fidelity\n257\t%% ============================================================\n258\t\n259\t\\begin{table}[t]\n260\t\\centering\n261\t\\caption{RSSM fidelity at $\\dt{=}1$ vs.\\ $\\dt{=}2$. Trained embedding (008d) produces $2.78\\times$ reward gap; removing it (008f) eliminates the gap but degrades absolute prediction.}\n262\t\\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}\n263\t\\footnotesize\n264\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n265\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n266\t\\toprule\n267\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{obs\\_MSE}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{reward\\_MAE}} \\\\\n268\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n269\t\\textbf{Config} & $\\dt{=}1$ & $\\dt{=}2$ & Gap & $\\dt{=}1$ & $\\dt{=}2$ & Gap \\\\\n270\t\\midrule\n271\tBaseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 0.0102 & 0.0159 & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n272\t008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\\times$ & 0.0568 & 0.1579 & 2.78$\\times$ \\\\\n273\t008f (no emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\\times$ & 0.7469 & 0.7526 & 1.01$\\times$ \\\\\n274\t\\bottomrule\n275\t\\end{tabular}\n276\t\\end{table}\n277\t\n278\t\\paragraph{RSSM fidelity analysis.}\n279\tTable~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity} reveals the mechanism underlying CIVO. The trained $\\dt$-embedding in 008d produces a $2.78\\times$ gap in reward prediction error between $\\dt{=}1$ and $\\dt{=}2$ trajectories. This asymmetric fidelity means the reward head systematically overpredicts rewards at $\\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates. In contrast, 008f (no embedding) shows a $1.01\\times$ gap---the reward head cannot distinguish temporal scales, which eliminates the exploitation channel but at the cost of severely degraded absolute reward prediction ($0.7469$ MAE vs.\\ $0.0568$ for 008d at $\\dt{=}1$).\n280\t\n281\t\\paragraph{Summary.}\n282\tF1--F5 establish the combination of trained $\\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor (7/8 classification, one non-CIVO false positive), with degradation scaling monotonically with $\\dt$.  F6 shows that restricting imagination to $\\dt{=}1$ prevents CIVO but not all collapse modes.  F7 demonstrates that full-pathway gradient stopping---removing the 92.7\\% observation decoder contribution while retaining the 7.3\\% KL signal---completely eliminates CIVO (eval 915.7, NVE $1.00\\times$), confirming that observation decoder gradients are necessary for CIVO induction.\n283\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QpsFyCKMn6rceLaDrtj1Cw","session_id":"a54be65d-94ff-4ee1-810f-f6483f74d2f7","uuid":"52862920-7c08-480a-9c48-81a8aaa4c03b","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:21:27.086Z"}
