{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WnEjtv6zHU2rLtHjUN2KN1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014p4P6fJGZ3e6EzZb5ggYFL","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T133210/best/paper.tex","offset":499,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23752,"cache_read_input_tokens":36981,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":23752,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012tcBpWjiorDLbm53E5QxcS","session_id":"a54be65d-94ff-4ee1-810f-f6483f74d2f7","uuid":"2e1a3437-5861-44b7-bf64-42ebfb244734"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014p4P6fJGZ3e6EzZb5ggYFL","type":"tool_result","content":"499\tThis confirms the causal chain (low-quality predictions $\\to$ value overestimation) and frames future work on smarter truncation policies.\n500\t\n501\t%% ============================================================\n502\t%% 5. DISCUSSION\n503\t%% ============================================================\n504\t\\section{Discussion}\n505\t\n506\t\\paragraph{The CIVO mechanism.}\n507\tOur results establish a complete causal chain: (1) temporal subsampling introduces dt$>$1 transitions into training data; (2) the observation-decoder gradient (93\\% of total) trains the dt-embedding to differentiate dt values; (3) the critic exploits this differentiation against inaccurate RSSM predictions at dt$>$1; (4) the resulting value overestimation (NVE$>$1.5$\\times$) drives the actor toward incorrect policies.\n508\tWhether overestimation becomes sustained (collapse) or transient (self-correction) depends on early training stochasticity---initialization and replay buffer sampling order determine whether the feedback loop locks in before corrective real-environment returns can counteract it.\n509\t\n510\t\\paragraph{Why TAWM succeeds but imagination fails.}\n511\tTAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} successfully uses dt-conditioning in TD-MPC2's MPC framework.\n512\tThe critical difference: MPC evaluates candidate action sequences against real environment outcomes, providing a corrective signal that prevents the overestimation feedback loop.\n513\tIn imagination-based actor-critic training, the actor and critic are trained entirely within the world model---there is no external correction during the imagination phase, allowing CIVO to compound unchecked.\n514\t\n515\t\\paragraph{Multiple vulnerability pathways.}\n516\tThe dual-pathway finding (Pathway A: CIVO, value-driven; Pathway B: eval-driven instability) reveals that trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through mechanisms beyond value overestimation.\n517\tPathway B operates even under dt$=$1 imagination: the trained dt-embedding receives gradient from real dt$>$1 transitions during world-model training, subtly corrupting the latent-state representation.\n518\tThis corruption destabilizes the actor without the classic value-explosion signature.\n519\tThe 1/4 Pathway B rate suggests this is a narrow failure mode requiring a specific conjunction of early training conditions.\n520\t\n521\t\\paragraph{Value self-correction.}\n522\tSeed 2 of the trained dt-embedding configuration (008d) demonstrates that NVE$>$1.5$\\times$ is necessary but not sufficient for collapse.\n523\tAfter peaking at NVE 2.05$\\times$, the value function self-corrected to 0.38$\\times$ by 105K while eval improved from 141 to 268.\n524\tThe bifurcation between sustained and transient overestimation likely depends on whether the actor's gradient from real returns can counteract the critic's bias before primacy effects lock it in.\n525\t\n526\t\\paragraph{Practical implications.}\n527\t\\begin{enumerate}\n528\t    \\item \\textbf{Do not train dt-embeddings} in imagination-based actor-critic without severing the observation-decoder gradient pathway.\n529\t    \\item \\textbf{NVE$>$1.5$\\times$ at 35K+} is a reliable early-warning diagnostic for CIVO (16/16 cross-environment accuracy).\n530\t    \\item \\textbf{dt$=$1 imagination} eliminates Pathway A but not Pathway B; the trained dt-embedding itself is the fundamental vulnerability.\n531\t    \\item \\textbf{Hierarchical approaches} (THICK, Director) that avoid flat dt-conditioning in the critic are the recommended direction for temporal abstraction in world-model RL.\n532\t\\end{enumerate}\n533\t\n534\t%% ============================================================\n535\t%% 6. CONCLUSION\n536\t%% ============================================================\n537\t\\section{Conclusion}\n538\t\n539\tWe presented a systematic diagnostic analysis of why temporal abstraction fails in DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic framework.\n540\tThe root cause is Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO): a trained dt-embedding, driven by observation-decoder gradients (93\\%), enables precise but systematically biased dt-conditioned critic predictions that compound through the imagination feedback loop.\n541\t\n542\tOur NVE diagnostic achieves 16/16 cross-environment classification accuracy at the 1.5$\\times$ threshold, and gradient decomposition pinpoints the exact architectural vulnerability.\n543\tThe finding that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC (TAWM) but fails in imagination localizes the problem to the actor-critic training loop, not temporal abstraction per se.\n544\t\n545\tThe dual-pathway characterization---CIVO (value-driven, entropy$\\downarrow$) and eval-driven instability (entropy$\\uparrow$)---demonstrates that surface-level mitigations are insufficient when the underlying trained dt-embedding corrupts the latent representation.\n546\tThese findings provide concrete, actionable guidance for future work on temporal abstraction in world-model reinforcement learning.\n547\t\n548\t%% ============================================================\n549\t%% REFERENCES\n550\t%% ============================================================\n551\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n552\t\n553\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2020)]{hafner2020dreamer}\n556\tD.~Hafner, T.~Lillicrap, J.~Ba, and M.~Norouzi.\n557\t\\newblock Dream to control: Learning behaviors by latent imagination.\n558\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2020.\n559\t\n560\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2021)]{hafner2021dreamerv2}\n561\tD.~Hafner, T.~Lillicrap, M.~Norouzi, and J.~Ba.\n562\t\\newblock Mastering Atari with discrete world models.\n563\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n564\t\n565\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2023)]{hafner2023dreamerv3}\n566\tD.~Hafner, J.~Pasukonis, J.~Ba, and T.~Lillicrap.\n567\t\\newblock Mastering diverse domains through world models.\n568\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2301.04104}, 2023.\n569\t\n570\t\\bibitem[Sutton et~al.(1999)]{sutton1999options}\n571\tR.~S. Sutton, D.~Precup, and S.~Singh.\n572\t\\newblock Between MDPs and semi-MDPs: A framework for temporal abstraction in reinforcement learning.\n573\t\\newblock \\emph{Artificial Intelligence}, 112(1--2):181--211, 1999.\n574\t\n575\t\\bibitem[Lakshminarayanan et~al.(2017)]{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic}\n576\tA.~S. Lakshminarayanan, S.~Sharma, and B.~Ravindran.\n577\t\\newblock Dynamic action repetition for deep reinforcement learning.\n578\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2017.\n579\t\n580\t\\bibitem[Sharma et~al.(2017)]{sharma2017figar}\n581\tS.~Sharma, A.~S. Lakshminarayanan, and B.~Ravindran.\n582\t\\newblock Fine grained action repetition for deep reinforcement learning.\n583\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:1702.06054}, 2017.\n584\t\n585\t\\bibitem[Micheli et~al.(2023)]{micheli2023iris}\n586\tV.~Micheli, E.~Alonso, and F.~Fleuret.\n587\t\\newblock Transformers are sample-efficient world models.\n588\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n589\t\n590\t\\bibitem[Robine et~al.(2023)]{robine2023twm}\n591\tJ.~Robine, M.~H{\\\"o}ftmann, T.~Uelwer, and S.~Harmeling.\n592\t\\newblock Transformer-based world models are happy with 100k interactions.\n593\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n594\t\n595\t\\bibitem[Nhu et~al.(2025)]{nhu2025tawm}\n596\tH.~Nhu et~al.\n597\t\\newblock Temporal abstraction in world models via MPC.\n598\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2025.\n599\t\n600\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2018)]{lu2018nondelusional}\n601\tT.~Lu, D.~Schuurmans, and C.~Boutilier.\n602\t\\newblock Non-delusional Q-learning and value iteration.\n603\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2018.\n604\t\n605\t\\bibitem[Janner et~al.(2019)]{janner2019mbpo}\n606\tM.~Janner, J.~Fu, M.~Zhang, and S.~Levine.\n607\t\\newblock When to trust your model: Model-based policy optimization.\n608\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2019.\n609\t\n610\t\\bibitem[Agarwal et~al.(2021)]{agarwal2021deep}\n611\tR.~Agarwal, M.~Schwarzer, P.~S. Castro, A.~Courville, and M.~G. Bellemare.\n612\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning at the edge of the statistical precipice.\n613\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2021.\n614\t\n615\t\\bibitem[Henderson et~al.(2018)]{henderson2018deep}\n616\tP.~Henderson, R.~Islam, P.~Bachman, J.~Pineau, D.~Precup, and D.~Meger.\n617\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning that matters.\n618\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2018.\n619\t\n620\t\\bibitem[Qiao et~al.(2023)]{qiao2023primacy}\n621\tZ.~Qiao et~al.\n622\t\\newblock Primacy bias in deep reinforcement learning.\n623\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2023.\n624\t\n625\t\\end{thebibliography}\n626\t\n627\t\\end{document}\n628\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012tcBpWjiorDLbm53E5QxcS","session_id":"a54be65d-94ff-4ee1-810f-f6483f74d2f7","uuid":"710c9631-10a6-4911-928f-40cfd6efeb28","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:21:30.143Z"}
