agile (the field has moved to hierarchy: THICK, Hieros, Director).\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n\n**Missing or under-cited:**\n1. **Palenicek et al. 2025 (\"Diminishing Return of Value Expansion Methods\")** — directly shows even oracle dynamics have diminishing returns with longer rollouts, supporting the value-estimation-not-model-accuracy thesis. Listed in literature_map but absent from paper.tex references.\n2. **Kim et al. 2025 (DEAS)** — independent evidence that temporal extension causes value overestimation in actor-critic. Different setting but same causal pattern.\n3. **Frauenknecht et al. 2025 (\"On Rollouts in Model-Based Reinforcement Learning\")** — ICLR 2025, directly studies imagination rollout divergence.\n4. **Tallec et al. 2019 (\"Making Deep Q-learning Robust to Time Discretization\")** — foundational theory on time-discretization sensitivity in value-based RL. Directly relevant.\n5. **Biedenkapp et al. 2021 (TempoRL)** — learns action persistence in model-free RL, relevant contrast.\n6. **DreamerV4 (Hafner et al. 2025)** — replaces RSSM with transformers. The paper's claims are RSSM-specific but doesn't discuss whether the mechanism transfers.\n7. **Li et al. 2025 (LS-Imagine, ICLR 2025 Oral)** — extends Dreamer imagination horizon via goal-conditioned jumps, directly relevant concurrent work.\n8. **MOPO (Yu et al. 2020)** — pessimistic reward penalty based on model uncertainty. The standard mitigation for model exploitation that the paper doesn't compare against.\n\n**Positioning fairness:**\n- The paper fairly differentiates from Lu et al.'s delusional bias.\n- The TAWM positioning is accurate but one-sided (only discusses why it succeeds, doesn't acknowledge TAWM may face CIVO-like issues in different regimes).\n- The paper undersells how much the pessimistic MBRL literature already addresses the general problem, making CIVO seem more isolated than it is.\n\n### Significance Assessment\n\n**Who benefits:**\n- Researchers attempting to add tempor